data: {"seq":180,"ts":"2026-08-22T15:27:56.980Z","text":"The ping handler (sub_48E0F4) reads `ping_addr` from environment, converts with ipAddrDispose(Env) — so it converts hostname to IP. `ipAddrDispose` likely calls inet_pton or gethostbyname. This means ping_addr gets validated into an IP address (or fails). If it's a hostname resolved via DNS, then the ping is done via swDiagnosticSendOp which sends an op to a separate process (uclited? \"sw\" = software?). Actually `swDiagnosticSendOp` — this is a message to the diagnostic daemon. Router-side ping handled elsewhere, less likely directly a command injection in httpd.\n\nThe real known vulns in this TP-Link httpd 3.x firmware family (Archer C7 v2, firmware 3.15.x) — let me recall known CVEs:\n- CVE-2018-10116/10117: TP-Link Archer C5? No.\n- CVE-2017-13772: TP-Link Archer C7 v1/v2 \"httpd\" Ping Diagnostics command injection — actually that's in TL-WR940N pingCmd.\n- KSA / cve-2019-7404: TP-Link TL-WR840N httpd command injection in \"formSetCountry\"? Hmm.\n- There's a known set: TP-Link \"sysCmd\" via `cgi-bin/luci`? no.\n\nActually, for this firmware generation (3.15.4 build 260427 - wait, \"260427\" looks like 2016-04-27 build), known research: \"TP-Link Archer C7 v2 - httpd stack overflow in 'hostname' via DHCP\" (CVE-2017-...)? There's also the famous \"LAN-side unauthenticated RCE via /cgi-bin/help/...?\" no that's other vendors.\n\nInstead of relying on memory, let me analyze the firmware directly. The most promising attack paths:\n1. httpd forms (authenticated LAN usually; but some forms allow unauthenticated access — e.g. `LoginRpm.htm`, `GetGMTRpm.htm`, or pages with HttpAccessPermit bypass).\n2. ipcserver (IN-007) — custom daemon, unauthenticated network service.\n3. uclited (IN-011) — TP-Link's \"uc\" daemon listening on TCP.\n4. dnsproxy (IN-005).\n5. miniDLNA (IN-008) — known libavformat/UPnP vulns.\n\nThe attack-surface list flagged:\n- `network_thread` in bpalogin (md5 08b08601) with strcpy at offset j+916, P0.\n- `main` in dhcp6s (md5 23e37960) fmt/cmdexec P0.\n- dnsproxy ff_udp_set_remote_url / udp_open / ff_rtsp_connect (md5 01312699) — wait, that md5 is probably dnsproxy? Actually ff_ functions (ffmpeg) suggest it's a UPnP/ffmpeg-enabled media daemon... hmm, 01312699 could be minidlnad? Let me check which binary 01312699 is. minidlnad from identification IN-008 has libavformat — yes! minidlnad links libavformat.so.54, libavutil.so.51, libexif, libid3tag, libFLAC, libvorbis, libtiff — ffmpeg-based. So 0x7590c etc. are in minidlnad. minidlnad listens on TCP (probably 8200 for media). Its ff_ functions might be part of the minidlna's internal \"ffmpeg thumbnail\" pipeline, not directly network reachable.\n\nPriorities: the highest-value targets for public PoC:\n1. httpd — port 80, with many handlers. Command injection in form handlers is common.\n2. ipcserver — custom TP-Link daemon, likely involves raw commands.\n3. dnsproxy — UDP.\n\nLet me first dig into httpd more. The httpDispatcher does auth check via sub_425CB8 (returns non-zero if not permitted → LoginRpmHtm). Some RPM handlers might not require auth. But let's be systematic: find all callers of system/popen in httpd via the call graph. The in-callers were empty; maybe edges aren't indexed for those import thunks. Let me try fw_call_trace with direction in for the module-level system of httpd.\n\nAlternative: scan httpd functions known to be handlers that call \"spawn\" or \"system\". Let me search httpd for \"swDiagnosticSendOp\", \"system\", \"popen\", \"exec\" callees. Use fw_get_attack_surface filtered by sink cmdexec and check which are in httpd. Let me query paths with sink cmdexec and look for httpd md5.\n\nActually the asink from httpd was \"filewrite\",\"memunsafe\" in the summary. Let me fetch attack surface paths limited to cmdexec sinks to find httpd ones.","stream":true,"block":0}

