data: {"seq":202,"ts":"2026-08-22T15:28:06.070Z","text":"Interesting — the cmdexec paths only show a few. The httpd md5 isn't included in cmdexec paths (maybe because httpd's cmdexec through system isn't connected via graph edges from network sources, since httpd handlers receive data via env).\n\nLet me take a different approach: look at uclited (IN-011). TP-Link's `uclited` is the \"universal config\" daemon that historically has command injection in `command` fields (the \"tdpServer\"/\"networkProcess\"). Actually, uclited is a daemon that listens on TCP 20005 (?) for \"uc\" protocol. And it has system at 0x5044e0 and popen at 0x504f10.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. TP-Link Archer C7v2 latest firmware... known CVEs for Archer C7 v2 (3.15.4):\n- CVE-2020-9376: TP-Link Archer C7 v2 firmware 3.15.4? — \"directory traversal in ... via cgi\" Hmm not sure.\n- Actually CVE-2020-9376 is Archer C7v2 LAN-side? Let me recall: \"TP-Link Archer C7v2 ... httpd has a stack-based buffer overflow in 'table_param' function\". Not certain.\n\nRather than memory, let me systematically look at uclited (custom daemon). First check identification for who runs uclited and its port. IN-011 says uclited binds tcp (port unknown). TP-Link uclited usually listens on 0.0.0.0:20005 for LAN management traffic from TP-Link app (tpLink Smart Connect / tether). Known historical CVE in Archer C7 v4/v5 uclited (CVE-2019-...? \"firmware: uclited command injection via 'command' in ...\"). Let me look at the uclited main.","stream":true,"block":0}

