data: {"seq":627,"ts":"2026-08-22T15:32:24.551Z","text":"Now I see the actual chain:\n- `sub_40F168(char *format, ...)` → vsprintf → `sub_40F0F8(\"echo \\\"====>>>>%s\\\" > /dev/ttyS0 \\r\\n\", s)` → vsprintf + system.\n\nIn get_duid: `sub_40F168(\"[%s]wan_if:%s\\n\", \"get_duid\", aEth02)` — aEth02 is a global (serial source). Not attacker-controlled in the DUID path.\n\nSo the dhcp6s system() chain is a debug/log echo to serial — format strings are compile-time constants. Not a vulnerability unless aEth02 etc. are attacker-controlled. aEth02 is the eth interface name from config. Not network.\n\nSo dhcp6s cmd path is a false positive. Good to know.\n\nbpalogin (l2tpd) start_pppd — execv pppd with args derived from tunnel config, not direct shell. Its `log_to_web` may format attacker strings... Let me look at log_to_web in bpalogin? Actually start_pppd calls log_to_web(\"pppoxl2tp fd2:%d\", v31). log_to_web likely logs to a file for the web.\n\nGiven the extensive survey, the strongest REAL target is **httpd** itself. Let me methodically find httpd's unauthenticated command injection. First, let me reconsider: which of the RPM pages are accessible without auth? In TP-Link httpd 3.x, all /userRpm/* URLs require a valid session EXCEPT those flagged. Actually wait — there's an important detail: **httpDispatcher processes `httpSystemRpms` first** and they run WITHOUT auth check. What's in httpSystemRpms? Let me find the initialization of that array. In httpd, the array httpSystemRpms is at some address; init function \"httpRpmSysAdd\"? Search for \"RpmSys\" or the string \"httpSystemRpmAdd\". Let me search strings: \"SystemRpm\" didn't match. Let me try \"RpmConfAdd\" (found httpRpmConfAdd) and see if there's an \"Add\" variant. Also inspect httpRpmFs@0x426880 — the fs router. And what registers into httpSystemRpms. Search for \"rpm\" in file names of httpd.","stream":false,"block":0}

