erflowing v8 writes the URL text over the stack. 511 bytes max. That's a genuine stack OOB write (CWE-121) with attacker-controlled length/contents, though bounded below return address. In MIPS with no stack canary (busybox builds usually no canary), an overflow that reaches $ra would give RCE. Need to check whether 511 bytes is enough. Frame: I need the assembly to determine saved-register offsets. From the decl, locals: dest sp+28, nptr sp+2C, v3 sp+30, i sp+34, v5 sp+38, v6 sp+3C, v7[16] sp+40, v8[7] sp+50, ... sp+0x25C, src sp+250, v14 sp+254, v15 sp+258. Stack frame likely sp+0x260 region + saved s0..s5 + ra + fp. If frame size is e.g. 0x2A0, saved registers at sp+0x260..0x29C, ra at ~sp+0x298-0x29C. URL max write from sp+0x50 to sp+0x50+511 = sp+0x20F — 0x50 bytes short of sp+0x25C... wait sp+0x50+511 = sp+0x24F (0x50+0x1FF). Hmm: 511 decimal = 0x1FF. 0x50+0x1FF = 0x24F. That's 0x11 bytes before src at 0x250. So it corrupts through v15 (0x258)... no: 0x50..0x24F covers v8(7) + v9 + v10 + v11 + v12(500 = 0x5C..0x248) + 0x50+0xFF? Let me recompute: v12 starts at sp+0x5C, 500 bytes → ends at sp+0x5C+500-1 = sp+0x24B. The URL write goes 0x50..0x24F → into src's first byte at 0x250? 0x24F+1=0x250. So no, it stops just before src. So the overflow covers v12 completely plus nothing else. Impact: v12 content controlled; saved regs untouched. Not control-flow hijack but still a stack OOB (CWE-121) with influence on downstream handlers that use v12.\n\nHmm, that weakens the finding. But wait — during the overflow we also clobber v9[2], v10, v11[2], which are passed BY VALUE? No — v9, v10, v11 are passed as pointers to SendResp_icon(a1, v9), SendResp_resizedimg(a1, &v10), SendResp_albumArt(a1, v11), SendResp_caption(a1, v11). Those pointers still point into the stack — the data in them","stream":true,"block":0}

