data: {"seq":1045,"ts":"2026-08-22T15:37:31.103Z","text":"sub_40D1FC parses the HTTP request. Key overflow candidates:\n1. `v8[7]` (7 bytes) — URL copied with `for (i=0; i<511 && *v5!=32 && *v5!=13; ++i) v8[i] = *v5++;` — WRITES UP TO 511 BYTES INTO A 7-BYTE STACK BUFFER v8!!! This is a clear stack overflow. The URL string (up to 511 chars) is copied into `char v8[7]` — stack overflow of ~504 bytes.\n\nWait — let me re-read. After parsing the method into v7[16]:\n```\nfor ( i = 0; i < 511 && *v5 != 32 && *v5 != 13; ++i )\n  v8[i] = *v5++;\nv8[i] = 0;\n```\nv8 is `char v8[7]` at [sp+50h], and right after it: v9[2] at +57, v10 at +59, v11[2] at +5A, v12[500] at +5Ch, src at +250... So writing beyond 7 bytes into v8 overflows into v9, v10, v11, v12 (500 bytes) — clobbering the 500-byte buffer used later by SendResp_thumbnail/SendResp_dlnafile(v12), and src pointer at +250. THIS IS A STACK BUFFER OVERFLOW in the DLNA HTTP request handling, triggered by a long URL — unauthenticated, LAN-reachable (DLNA port), no session needed!\n\nHold on — let me double check the buffer boundaries. v8 is char[7] at sp+0x50. The loop writes up to 511 bytes → overflow into v9[2] (sp+57), v10 (sp+59), v11[2] (sp+5A), v12[500] (sp+5C..sp+250), src (sp+250)... The overflow writes attacker-controlled URL bytes (from *v5, the HTTP request buffer) across 500+ bytes of stack. That will corrupt v12 (used as output buffer for thumbnail/dlnafile) and the `src` pointer (local). If we overflow far enough (511 bytes), we overwrite:\n- v12[500]: bytes 0x5C..0x248 — all 500 bytes.\n- src at sp+250 (4 bytes), v14[2] sp+254, v15 sp+258 — up to 511 bytes total from sp+50 → up to sp+0x50+511=sp+0x20F — only reaches just past v15.\n\nBut wait — after the overflow, does the code still use v8 as a URL? Yes: `if (strcmp(\"/rootDesc.xml\", v8))` etc. — multiple strcmps against the corrupted v8. The overflow writes 0x00 terminator at v8[i]. It may corrupt saved $s0/$ra? The frame: locals at sp+28..sp+25C; saved registers would be above (sp+260+). 511 bytes from sp+0x50 → sp+0x20F — does NOT reach saved regs (need to know frame size; a1 is arg). Actually the loop limit 511 from v8 start (sp+50) reaches sp+0x20F; the saved registers are at higher addresses (from the fp we can estimate: this function has only a1 param; locals end at sp+25C; saved regs at sp+260 +; overflow max = 511+50 = sp+0x21F... just misses them. But it corrupts `src` (sp+250), `v14[2]` (sp+254), `v15` (sp+258), and `v6`? v6 is a local at sp+3C (below v7, written before). Hmm wait — v6 at sp+3C: that's the HTTP version copy target `v6 = a1+16` — no.\n\nSo the practical impact: massive stack clobbering of a 500-byte buffer + pointer `src` (used immediately in the (a1+116 & 0x100) chunked-request path — no wait, that branch happens BEFORE the URL parse? Let me re-check ordering: after copying v8 and HTTP version, the code at \"if ((*(_DWORD *)(a1+116)&0x100)!=0)\" processes chunked POST body... using src computed from v3 = request_buffer + offset. That happens right after the copy of the URL into v8! So the corrupted `src` local (sp+250) value gets used after the overflow in the chunked processing: `nptr = (char*)src + *(a1+112)` — attack controls the `src` pointer → arbitrary read/write... but only if the (a1+116 & 0x100) flag (chunked) is set.\n\nMore robustly: the overflow is a stack buffer overflow with partial control → at least memory corruption / DoS; with 511-byte limit it stops before $ra/$s0, so it's likely \"only\" corruption of locals — limited control-flow impact. Hmm. But v12 (500-byte buffer at sp+5C) fully overwritten with attacker URL data, then \n- if URL starts with \"/Thumbnails/\" → SendResp_thumbnail(a1, v12) uses v12 as the parsed value buffer — attacker controls v12 content (their URL bytes) — v12 will hold part of the request URL... this is data-only.\n\nWait, actually the loop copies from *v5 — the pointer advanced past method+space. v5 points at the URL in the request buffer. So overflowing v8 writes the URL text over the stack. 511 bytes max. That's a genuine stack OOB write (CWE-121) with attacker-controlled length/contents, though bounded below return address. In MIPS with no stack canary (busybox builds usually no canary), an overflow that reaches $ra would give RCE. Need to check whether 511 bytes is enough. Frame: I need the assembly to determine saved-register offsets. From the decl, locals: dest sp+28, nptr sp+2C, v3 sp+30, i sp+34, v5 sp+38, v6 sp+3C, v7[16] sp+40, v8[7] sp+50, ... sp+0x25C, src sp+250, v14 sp+254, v15 sp+258. Stack frame likely sp+0x260 region + saved s0..s5 + ra + fp. If frame size is e.g. 0x2A0, saved registers at sp+0x260..0x29C, ra at ~sp+0x298-0x29C. URL max write from sp+0x50 to sp+0x50+511 = sp+0x20F — 0x50 bytes short of sp+0x25C... wait sp+0x50+511 = sp+0x24F (0x50+0x1FF). Hmm: 511 decimal = 0x1FF. 0x50+0x1FF = 0x24F. That's 0x11 bytes before src at 0x250. So it corrupts through v15 (0x258)... no: 0x50..0x24F covers v8(7) + v9 + v10 + v11 + v12(500 = 0x5C..0x248) + 0x50+0xFF? Let me recompute: v12 starts at sp+0x5C, 500 bytes → ends at sp+0x5C+500-1 = sp+0x24B. The URL write goes 0x50..0x24F → into src's first byte at 0x250? 0x24F+1=0x250. So no, it stops just before src. So the overflow covers v12 completely plus nothing else. Impact: v12 content controlled; saved regs untouched. Not control-flow hijack but still a stack OOB (CWE-121) with influence on downstream handlers that use v12.\n\nHmm, that weakens the finding. But wait — during the overflow we also clobber v9[2], v10, v11[2], which are passed BY VALUE? No — v9, v10, v11 are passed as pointers to SendResp_icon(a1, v9), SendResp_resizedimg(a1, &v10), SendResp_albumArt(a1, v11), SendResp_caption(a1, v11). Those pointers still point into the stack — the data in them is now attacker-controlled URL bytes (kept NUL-terminated at v8[i] which is beyond). E.g., SendResp_icon(a1, v9) reads icon path from v9[2] (2 bytes)... the attacker's URL bytes at v9 region (inherited from overflow) become the \"icon\" path arg → might be used in realpath/strcpy into a file path → could lead to arbitrary file access. Interesting but messy.\n\nGiven v12 is used as URL query parsing buffer in SendResp_dlnafile/SendResp_thumbnail (they parse \"?albumID=xxx&objectID=...\" style), and SendResp_thumbnail(a1, v12) will treat v12 as the URL \"%6dx%6d\" (thumbnail request format) — attacker URL bytes flow into sscanf(\"%6dx%6d\") and file path construction → potential file read/DoS but not code exec.\n\nThis is still a legit unauthenticated memory-corruption finding (C","stream":true,"block":0}

